"transcription.text": {
"name": "transcription.text",
"value": "A quantum computer is a digital computer capable of exploding quantum\r\ncurrents among the physical two-state systems that store the binary arithmetic information.\r\nThe factoring integral is to find its expression as a product of prime numbers.\r\nThe most impressive, most important and best known thing about a quantum computer\r\nthat it can do is to factor its spectacular efficiency, the product of two enormous prime numbers.\r\nBut what an earth can quantum mechanics have to do with factoring.\r\nThis question bothered me for four years from that time I heard about the discovery\r\nthat a quantum computer is spectacularly good at factoring until I finally took the\r\ntrouble to find out how it was done.\r\nAnd the answer you will be relieved but if you were like me also disappointed to learn\r\nis that quantum mechanics has nothing at all to do with factoring.\r\nBut it doesn\u0027t have to do with waves.\r\nPretty important ways of periodic so it is not very surprising that quantum mechanics\r\nmight be useful in efficient and revealing features associated with periodicity.\r\nQuantum mechanics is connected to factoring through periodicity.\r\nIt turns out purely arithmetic reasons are having nothing to do with quantum mechanics.\r\nThat if we have an efficient way to find the period of a periodic function then as we\r\nshall discuss we can easily factor the product of two enormous prime numbers.\r\nI have quantum computer provides an extremely efficient way to find periods.\r\nThis is of considerable practical importance because the great difficulty in factoring\r\nsuch a product where the two enormous prime numbers are typically each several hundred\r\ndays long is the basis for the security of the most widely used encryption scheme for\r\nprotecting private information sent over the internet.\r\nIn 1994 and 5, a pretty sure discovered that a quantum computer will be super efficient\r\nat period finding and thereby it was a potential threat to innumerable secrets.\r\nWent to explosion interest in developing quantum computation.\r\nThe threat is only potential because no quantum computer capable of anything like serious\r\nperiod finding actually exists.\r\nI suspect the emphasis of being put on factoring rather than period finding because factoring\r\nis more famously associated with the RSA code breaking.\r\nAlthough as it happens period finding can be used directly to crack the RSA code without\r\nany need for a due to an entire pattern.\r\nFactoring is also a mathematical concept more familiar to the general public than period\r\nfinding.\r\nFor instance the focus on factoring has led to some spectacular misinterpretations of\r\nSchor\u0027s algorithm or what Einstein called secular press.\r\nFor example the New York Times Science writer George Johnson says in his book on quantum\r\ncomputation the Schor\u0027s got through time that when the algorithm has done its job the\r\nsolutions, the factors of the number being analyzed, will all be in superposition.\r\nElsewhere he says that a quantum computer can try out all possible factors simultaneously\r\nin superposition, think elapsed revealed the answer.\r\nNeither of these things bears a status of resonance to what the algorithm actually does.",
